WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.440
All right.

00:00.440 --> 00:05.840
So previously we have understood that we can put all our SQL statements in one stored procedure.

00:05.840 --> 00:09.560
And you have to go and manually execute the stored procedure.

00:09.560 --> 00:15.160
So that means in order to trigger the stored procedure you have manually to execute it.

00:15.160 --> 00:16.720
And this is of course a problem.

00:16.720 --> 00:19.080
How about do that automatically.

00:19.080 --> 00:26.800
So triggers in SQL they are special stored procedure that automatically runs or let's say fired in response

00:26.800 --> 00:29.920
to a specific event that happens on a table.

00:29.920 --> 00:31.520
So what this exactly means.

00:31.520 --> 00:34.480
So now let's say that we have a table in our database.

00:34.520 --> 00:40.320
And now something could happen to this table like inserting data, deleting updating data, all that

00:40.440 --> 00:41.720
stuff that is happening.

00:41.720 --> 00:43.040
We call them events.

00:43.240 --> 00:47.720
And now what we can do, we can go and attach like a trigger on top of this table.

00:47.720 --> 00:53.440
And each time an event happened like insert, update, delete, something else can be triggered, like

00:53.440 --> 00:58.240
maybe going and inserting data somewhere else in another table or doing a check.

00:58.240 --> 01:03.520
Whether we are allowed to delete the data in the first place, or maybe sending a warning message or

01:03.520 --> 01:04.200
something.

01:04.200 --> 01:10.700
So based on any changes to the table, we can trigger another event and we can do that using the SQL

01:10.740 --> 01:11.420
triggers.

01:11.420 --> 01:16.380
And for the SQL triggers we have like multiple types like the DML triggers.

01:16.380 --> 01:21.540
And this type of trigger can respond once we have like insert update delete statements.

01:21.580 --> 01:27.700
Another type of triggers we have the DDL triggers like you can make a trigger to respond to any schema

01:27.700 --> 01:32.340
changes like creating, altering or dropping a table or even view.

01:32.340 --> 01:33.660
By the way, not only tables.

01:33.660 --> 01:39.300
And the third type of triggers, we have the login trigger so the trigger can respond to a login events.

01:39.340 --> 01:43.740
Now in this tutorial we're going to focus on the DML triggers, the insert, update delete.

01:43.740 --> 01:46.740
And for the DML triggers we have two types.

01:46.740 --> 01:48.460
We have after triggers.

01:48.500 --> 01:51.420
And as well we have instead of triggers.

01:51.420 --> 01:54.060
So as the name suggests if you use after.

01:54.220 --> 01:56.900
So it's going to be executed after the event.

01:56.940 --> 02:01.620
And the other type that instead of it's something that cannot wait until everything happens.

02:01.620 --> 02:06.900
So this time the trigger can be executed during the event, not after it.

02:06.940 --> 02:10.860
So now in order to understand all of this, we're going to have really nice use case.
